§10 expressiveness results.


1. What a Reading is
--------------------

A Reading is a recorded path of attention through the knowledge graph.

    R = (G(t), P)

  - t     is a lambda term (the record of the path taken so far).
  - G(t)  is the shared graph representing t — a sharing graph in the sense
          of the GAL bus algorithm (see the `optimal_lambda/` package).
  - P     is a set of pointers. Each pointer designates a node or an edge
          of G(t) — a position the reader may act at next.

The reader is never "outside" the reading: at all times the live positions are
exactly the members of P. A reading with |P| > 1 records attention that has
been forked and not yet collapsed.


2. Types are entities
---------------------

Typing here is NOT the typing of the usual typed lambda calculus. It is a
device for answering one question: WHERE IS THE READER STANDING?

  - Types are exactly entities of the knowledge graph. Written A, B, C, ...
  - Variables are written a, b, c, ...  Each variable has a type [a].
  - [t] denotes the type of the term t.

Typing rules:

    [a]           = A            for a variable a of type A
    [app(a, b)]   = [b]
    [(lam a) t]   = [a]

The second rule is the load-bearing one. Applying the current term to b moves
the type to [b] — so extending the term along a relation A->B leaves the term
with type B. THE TYPE OF THE TERM IS THE ENTITY THE READER CURRENTLY OCCUPIES.
Reading a term is therefore reading a trajectory: each application is one step,
and the type after each step says where that step landed.

The third rule gives an abstraction the type of its BOUND VARIABLE (not of its
body).

Correspondence to code: `term_utils._term_type` already implements exactly
these three rules and is treated here as normative.

An arrow A->B denotes a relation (claim) of the knowledge graph with subject A
and object B. [ASSUMPTION] Selecting an arrow is what licenses a step: the
reader may only move along relations that actually exist in the graph.
(cf. `kg_store.get_claims_for_subject`.)


3. Initial step
---------------

A reading begins in exactly one of two ways.

  (init-1) START AT AN ENTITY.
      The user selects an arbitrary entity A. This means the reader stands at
      A. The reading is of type A.

          t = a,  where [a] = A
          R = (G(a), {p}),  p pointing at the whole of G(a)

  (init-2) RESUME A SAVED READING.
      The user selects an already saved reading R1 = (G, P) and then selects
      one pointer p from P.

          R = R1, with p as the acting position

      Reading then continues exactly as in the generic case below: init-2 is
      not a special mode, it merely chooses the starting reading and position.

Note the kinship with contraction case 2 (§4.2): both "enter" a saved reading
and adopt its pointers. They differ in that init-2 starts from R1 alone, with
no prior term or pointers to carry, whereas case 2 performs the same entry
mid-reading — grafting R1 onto the accumulated term and taking Pr in ADDITION
to the reader's surviving positions.


3b. The governing design principle: the reader does not know the graph
----------------------------------------------------------------------

Stated by Marek, 2026-07-29. This is the constraint from which the shape of the
whole operation set follows, so it is recorded before the operations.

  THE READER DOES NOT KNOW THE KNOWLEDGE GRAPH IN ADVANCE.
  Every operand of every step must therefore be something the reader ALREADY
  KNOWS — either what is visible from where it now stands, or its own prior
  readings.

Two admissible sources of knowledge, and nothing else:

  (K1) LOCAL VISIBILITY. Standing at an entity A, the reader can see the arrows
       incident to A — outgoing A -> X and incoming X -> A — and nothing
       further. It cannot name an entity two hops away, because it has not
       been there.

  (K2) ITS OWN READINGS. A saved reading is the reader's own prior work. Its
       term and its positions are fully known to it, however far they range
       over the graph.

EVERY STEP IS LOCAL. Each acts at one pointer p and uses only an arrow
incident to the type at p, plus possibly one saved reading grafted there. No
step ranges over the graph, and no step takes two readings as co-equal
operands. (The former case 5 did; it was removed for exactly this reason —
§4.2d.)

Consequences, which explain the operation set as designed rather than
arbitrary:

  - Cases 1, 2 and 4 are LOCAL because of (K1): each acts at one pointer and
    uses only an arrow incident to the type at that pointer. Locality is not
    an end in itself here; it is what (K1) forces when the graph is unknown.

  - (K2) enters only THROUGH a pointer: cases 2 and 4 graft a saved reading at
    the position the reader already occupies. The reading's content may range
    arbitrarily over the graph — that is fine, it is the reader's own — but the
    ACT of grafting is still local.

  - A general "navigate to an arbitrary position / arbitrary entity" step would
    be INADMISSIBLE. It would let the reader designate a position it has never
    read, which presupposes exactly the graph knowledge (K1) denies. Note this
    is not needed for expressiveness either: grafting saved readings at a
    pointer already yields every application shape (§10.1).

  - It also explains C1 (§9), i.e. why case 2 adopts Pr while case 4 discards
    it. Entering a saved reading (argument position) means inheriting positions
    the reader has itself previously stood at — admissible under (K2). A
    reading placed behind the reader (function position) is context, not a
    place the reader occupies, so there are no positions to inherit.

  - THE INITIAL STEP IS THE ONLY PLACE GRAPH KNOWLEDGE ENTERS. init-1 lets the
    user select an arbitrary entity A with no prior reading to justify it;
    init-2 selects a saved reading, which is (K2). So init-1 is the single
    admitted breach, and presumably corresponds to search/browse — a facility
    OUTSIDE the reading calculus by which the reader first comes to know an
    entity at all. Everything after the initial step is (K1) or (K2), and every
    step after it is local.


4. Generic definition
---------------------

After the initial step, a Reading is a SEQUENCE of steps, each of which is one
of two operations:

    - contraction step   (three cases: 1 §4.1, 2 §4.2, 4 §4.2c)
    - reflection step    (§4.3)

Two cases of an earlier draft are gone: case 3 is omitted as a special case of
case 4 (§4.2b), and case 5 is removed to keep every step local (§4.2d). The
numbering 1, 2, 4 is kept so earlier notes still refer to the same cases.

ALL of them act on the current reading R = (G(t), P) at one chosen pointer p
from P, using only knowledge admitted by §3b. There are no exceptions: every
step is local.


4.1 Contraction step, case 1 — follow a relation
------------------------------------------------

The user selects a relation  A -> B.

  Precondition:
      p points to a subgraph G(t1) with [t1] == A.

  Effect:
      G(t2) is G(t) with the subgraph G(t1) replaced by G(app(t1, b)),
      where b is a fresh variable with [b] == B.

      P1 is P with p replaced by a pointer to G(app(t1, b)).

      R := (G(t2), P1)

  Resulting type at that pointer:  [app(t1,b)] = [b] = B.
  The reader has moved from A to B. All other pointers of P are untouched.

This is the ordinary "step along an edge" of a reading.


4.2 Contraction step, case 2 — enter a saved reading
-----------------------------------------------------

The user selects  A -> R1,  where R1 = (G(tr1), Pr) is an already saved
reading.

  Precondition:
      p points to a subgraph G(t1) with [t1] == A.

  Effect:
      G(t2) is G(t) with the subgraph G(t1) replaced by G(app(t1, R1)).

      P1 = Pr  union  (P - p)

      R := (G(t2), P1)

  THE READER STEPS INSIDE R1. The saved reading's own pointers Pr become live
  positions of the current reading. This is the defining property of case 2:
  grafting a saved reading is a DESCENT into it, not merely an attachment
  below the current position.

  Consequences, stated explicitly:
      - p is CONSUMED by the graft: it does not survive into P1. (It is the
        position that was spent to enter R1.)
      - every OTHER pointer of P does survive — the reader keeps whatever
        positions it already had elsewhere.
      - the reading gains all of R1's positions at once, so |P| may grow by
        more than one.
      - the term G(t2) records the whole path, including everything traversed
        before the descent.

  So case 1 moves one position along an edge; case 2 spends one position to
  open up all of R1's positions, while keeping the accumulated term and the
  reader's other positions.

  [ASSUMPTION] The argument grafted in argument position is the saved
  reading R1 as a whole (term together with its pointers), which is why Pr is
  meaningful in the result. Whether G(tr1) is COPIED into G(t2) or SHARED with
  the stored R1 via a fan-in is not fixed by the specification. Copying makes
  each graft independent; sharing makes later edits of R1 visible in every
  reading that grafted it. The pseudocode in `reading_alg` parameterises this
  as a flag `SHARE_GRAFTED_READING` and defaults to COPY, since copy keeps
  saved readings immutable and requires only that Pr be rebased onto the copy.


4.2b Case 3 — OMITTED (subsumed by case 4)
-------------------------------------------

An earlier version of this specification had a case 3: "user selects A -> B,
p points to G(t1) with [t1] == B, replace G(t1) by G(app(a, t1)) where
[a] == A, and re-point p at the new application."

IT IS OMITTED as a primitive, being an exact special case of case 4 (§4.2c).
Instantiate case 4 at the TRIVIAL READING

    R_a = ( G(a), {ptr(a)} )   with a a single variable, [a] == A

and case 4 produces app(tr1, t1) = app(a, t1) — the same term, the same
precondition [t1] == B, the same resulting type B, and the same pointer
result. The collapse works precisely BECAUSE case 4 discards Pr (§9 C1): case 3
also leaves P untouched apart from re-pointing p, so nothing distinguishes
them.

The case numbering 1, 2, 4, 5 is kept as-is so that this document and any
earlier notes refer to the same cases by the same numbers.

For contrast, case 1 is NOT a special case of case 2, even though the terms
likewise coincide for a trivial reading. Case 2 yields Pr union (P - p),
consuming p and adopting the grafted reading's positions, whereas case 1
re-points p at the new application node. Instantiating case 2 at a trivial
R_b = (G(b), {ptr(b)}) would leave the pointer on the ARGUMENT b, not on
app(t1, b). The descent semantics of case 2 is the obstruction — see §5.

Consequence for implementations: since arriving from a plain entity is the
common move and arriving from a saved reading the rarer one, an implementation
should offer "arrive from entity A" in the interface and realise it by
SYNTHESISING the trivial reading R_a and invoking case 4. See §4.2c.


4.2c Contraction step, case 4 — arrive from a saved reading R1 -> B
-------------------------------------------------------------------

The dual of case 2: a saved reading is placed in FUNCTION position, so the
reader arrives at B from R1. Subsumes the former case 3 (§4.2b) when R1 is a
trivial single-variable reading.

The user selects  R1 -> B,  where R1 = (G(tr1), Pr) is an already saved
reading.

  Precondition:
      p points to a subgraph G(t1) with [t1] == B.

  Effect:
      G(t2) is G(t) with the subgraph G(t1) replaced by G(app(tr1, t1)).

      P1 is P with p replaced by a pointer to G(app(tr1, t1)).

      R := (G(t2), P1)

  Resulting type at that pointer:  [app(tr1,t1)] = [t1] = B — UNCHANGED.
  This follows from [app(a,b)] = [b]: the type is carried by the ARGUMENT, so
  wrapping t1 in a new function position leaves the reader's location alone.
  Where case 1 MOVES the reader A -> B, case 4 keeps the reader at B and
  enriches the record with where it arrived FROM. Attention does not travel;
  context accumulates behind it.

  ARRIVING FROM A PLAIN ENTITY. To record arrival at B from a bare entity A
  (the former case 3), instantiate this case at the trivial reading

      R_a = ( G(a), {ptr(a)} ),   a a single variable with [a] == A

  giving app(a, t1). Since Pr is discarded, the trivial reading's pointer
  contributes nothing and the result is exactly the omitted case 3 (§4.2b).
  An implementation should expose this as its own interface affordance —
  arriving from an entity is the common move — while realising it through this
  single primitive.

  Note what is grafted and what is NOT adopted: the saved reading's TERM tr1
  goes into function position, but its pointer set Pr is DISCARDED — P1 comes
  from P alone. This is the exact opposite of case 2. It is CONFIRMED correct
  (see §9 C1) and it is coherent:

      case 2  puts R1 in ARGUMENT position; the type becomes [tr1], so the
              reader ENTERS R1 and takes on its positions Pr.
      case 4  puts R1 in FUNCTION position; the type stays B, so the reader
              REMAINS where it was and R1 becomes recorded context behind it.
              There is nothing to enter, hence no reason to adopt Pr.

  Position in argument vs. function is thus what decides both the type and the
  fate of the pointers — a single principle covering every case.

  All other pointers of P are untouched; |P| is unchanged.


4.2d Case 5 — REMOVED from the specification (kept as a future enhancement)
---------------------------------------------------------------------------

Removed by Marek, 2026-07-29, to make EVERY step local (§3b). Recorded here
because it may be reinstated later.

What it was: a step stated not for a reading and a pointer, but for TWO
READINGS —

      given  R1 = (G(t1), P1)  and  R2 = (G(t2), P2):
      R := ( G(app(t1, t2)), P2 )

i.e. apply R1's term to R2's term and keep R2's pointers. (Consistent with the
argument-determines-everything principle of §5: [app(t1,t2)] = [t2], so the
reader ends up wherever R2 pointed. It was case 2 with the host reduced to
nothing.)

WHY REMOVING IT COSTS NOTHING. Case 5 was the only non-local step — the only
one whose operands were two whole readings rather than one pointer and its
surroundings. Removing it makes the epistemic principle of §3b uniform: every
remaining step uses only (K1), what is visible from the current position, or
grafts a saved reading at a pointer.

Crucially this loses NO expressive power. Cases 2 and 4 already graft a saved
reading's ENTIRE TERM at a pointer, and a saved reading may itself be
arbitrarily compound. Application shapes therefore remain COMPLETE without
case 5 — verified by exhaustive enumeration to 6 leaves (§10.1). For example
((x y) (z w)) is built by saving (z w) as a reading and grafting it at a
pointer inside (x y), with no need to join two readings.

So case 5 was REDUNDANT, not load-bearing. It offered a convenience — compose
two finished readings directly — at the cost of the only breach of locality in
the calculus.

IF REINSTATED, the two points left open about it were: whether it produces a
new reading or modifies one in place (the original text said "R is modified"
but named no pointer and no host subgraph), and whether R1 == R2 is permitted.


4.3 Reflection step — fork attention over a shared subject
-----------------------------------------------------------

The user selects  B -> C.

  Precondition:
      p points to a subgraph G(tA) with [tA] == A.

  Effect:
      G(t2) is G(t) with the subgraph G(tA) replaced by

          G( app( app(tA, B), app(tA, C) ) )

      where tA IS SHARED BY A FAN-IN — it occurs twice in the term but exists
      once in the graph.

      P1 is P with p removed and two new pointers added:
          pB  points to the edge ending on the UPPER LEFT auxiliary port of
              the sharing fan-in;
          pC  points to the edge ending on the UPPER RIGHT auxiliary port of
              the sharing fan-in.

      R := (G(t2), P1)

  Where contraction MOVES the reader, reflection SPLITS the reader: one
  position continues under B, the other under C, over one and the same
  subject tA. Sharing is essential — the two branches must be two views of the
  same thing, not two copies of it. This is exactly the sharing that the
  optimal reducer provides.

  [ASSUMPTION — types] The relation of A to B and C is not fixed by the
  original text: it says the user selects "B->C" while the pointer carries
  type A, and A then never recurs. The reading adopted here is that reflection
  contrasts two relations OUT OF the pointed subject, i.e. the user in effect
  selects A->B and A->C, and "B->C" names the contrasted pair of targets. Under
  that reading the two branches have types B and C respectively.
  If instead a single relation B->C is meant with A constrained to equal B,
  the only change needed is the precondition ([tA] == B) and the type of the
  second branch.

  [ASSUMPTION — operands] The original text writes app(tA, B) and app(tA, C)
  with the ENTITIES B and C in argument position, whereas contraction case 1
  carefully applies to a fresh VARIABLE b with [b] == B. For consistency this
  document takes reflection to build app(tA, b) and app(tA, c) with fresh
  variables b, c of types B and C. This keeps a single discipline — argument
  positions hold variables, types are carried by the typing judgement — and
  makes [app(tA,b)] = B fall out of the ordinary rule.

  Correspondence to code: the sharing fan-in built by `_Compiler._fan_in`
  (`optimal_lambda/compile.py`) is an arity-3 INTERNAL fan marked on the OFFSET
  wire, with ports named `principal` / `grey` / `black`. "Upper left aux port"
  and "upper right aux port" are `grey` and `black` respectively; `principal`
  carries the shared subject tA. The OFFSET placement is what makes the two
  uses DUPLICATE rather than annihilate when they meet the syntactic
  lambda/application fans on the COMMAND wire — see the sibling skill
  `understand-bus-optimal-reduction` §3.


5. Summary of the operations
----------------------------

  Contraction has THREE primitive cases: 1, 2 and 4. Case 3 is omitted as a
  special case of case 4 (§4.2b); case 5 is removed to keep every step local
  (§4.2d). The numbering is preserved.

  The three are exactly the cells of one 2x2 grid, minus the cell that
  collapses — two choices: what is grafted, and where it goes:

                            argument position      function position
      fresh variable        case 1  (depart)        [collapses into case 4]
      saved reading         case 2  (descend)       case 4  (arrive from)

  There is no outlier. Every case grafts material at one pointer.

  The single governing principle, from [app(a,b)] = [b]: THE ARGUMENT
  DETERMINES BOTH THE RESULTING TYPE AND THE SURVIVING POINTERS. Put material
  in argument position and the reader moves into it; put it in function
  position and the reader stays put while the material becomes context behind
  it.

  Note the hole in the upper-right cell, and why only THAT cell collapses. A
  fresh variable differs from a saved reading only in having no pointers of its
  own. In FUNCTION position the grafted material's pointers are discarded
  anyway, so the distinction is invisible and case 3 reduces to case 4. In
  ARGUMENT position they are adopted, so the distinction is load-bearing and
  case 1 does NOT reduce to case 2.

                 case 1        case 2         case 4
  selected       A -> B        A -> R1        R1 -> B
  required [t1]  A             A              B
  grafts         app(t1,b)     app(t1,R1)     app(tr1,t1)
  type after     B             [tr1]          B (unchanged)
  pointers       p re-pointed  p -> Pr,       p re-pointed
                               rest kept      (Pr dropped)
  |P| changes    0             -1 + |Pr|      0
  reader         departs       descends       arrives
                 A -> B        into R1        from R1

  Reflection (§4.3) stands apart from all three: it is the only operation that
  SHARES its subject (fan-in) and the only one that INCREASES the number of
  positions, p -> {pB, pC}.

  In every operation, contraction and reflection alike, exactly ONE pointer (p)
  is acted on and every other pointer of P is left untouched.

  Cases 1 and 4 are the two ways one arrow can be used, distinguished by where
  the reader stands: case 1 fires when the reader is at the SUBJECT and moves
  it to the object; case 4 fires when the reader is at the OBJECT and records
  the source behind it without moving. Their preconditions differ ([t1]==A vs
  [t1]==B), so a given arrow and pointer generally admit only one of them —
  both only when A == B (a reflexive relation).


6. Invariants
-------------

  I1  Every pointer of P designates a node or edge of the CURRENT G(t).
      Every step must re-establish this for pointers it touches.

  I2  EVERY step acts at exactly one pointer p of P, and leaves every pointer
      other than p untouched. Contraction cases 1 and 4 re-point p; case 2
      consumes p and adds Pr; reflection consumes p and adds pB, pC. There is
      no exception (the former case 5, which acted at no pointer, is removed —
      §4.2d).

  I3  The type at a pointer is well defined: for a pointer to G(t1), [t1] is
      computed by the three rules of §2.

  I4  A step is legal only if its precondition on [t1] holds; the type at the
      pointer is what selects which case is available:

          case 1  arrow A -> B      requires [t1] == A
          case 2  arrow A -> R1     requires [t1] == A
          case 4  arrow R1 -> B     requires [t1] == B

      A mismatch is not a legal move.

  I5  Reflection never duplicates its subject: after the step there is exactly
      one G(tA) in the graph, reached through the fan-in.

  I6  P is never empty after a legal step. Contraction cases 1 and 4 preserve
      the count; reflection turns one pointer into two; case 2 yields
      Pr union (P - p), which is empty only in the degenerate case where
      P == {p} and Pr is empty. Requiring saved readings to have non-empty
      pointer sets rules that out.


7. Ontology
-----------

Consolidated from the draft given by Marek, 2026-07-29, with the four points
he clarified in that session marked [CLARIFIED].

7.1 What an ontology is, and why
---------------------------------

A READING records what the user DID. An ONTOLOGY is a MODEL IN WHICH THAT
READING IS VALID.

The division of labour is the point of the design:

    reading   = the observation. A bare applicative term: NO abstractions
                (§10.1), built only from arrows the reader could see (§3b).
    ontology  = the theory. A term that DOES contain abstractions, such that
                reducing it in the bus reducer REPLAYS the reading step for
                step.
    reducer   = the test. Each reading step must be matched by the
                corresponding rule firing in the ontology.

The correspondence between reading steps and reduction rules is exact:

    contraction in the reading   <->   application of R1 (fan/fan same wire:
                                       annihilate — the shared beta-step)
    reflection  in the reading   <->   application of R4 (fan/fan different
                                       wires: duplicate)

So an ontology is a hypothesis about the SCHEMA the reader is implicitly
walking, and the ontology SET is every hypothesis still consistent with the
reading so far. This is why Reading itself needs no abstractions: abstraction
lives entirely in the ontology layer.

Notation, mirroring a reading:

    ont( G(t), P )

where G(t) is a shared graph and P is a set of pointers marking where the user
is standing IN THIS GRAPH. P corresponds elementwise to the reading's own
pointer set — each p in the reading has a counterpart p1 in each ontology.

Each reading has a SET of ontologies. They are maintained continuously: after
every reading step the set is updated (§7.4-7.6) and then enriched (§7.3).


7.2 Questions
-------------

[CLARIFIED] A QUESTION is a lambda abstraction created by the user from a
reading, by selecting the entity being questioned. This is already implemented:

    app.py           binding "a" -> action_lambda_abstraction ("Ask question")
    widgets.py       LambdaAbstractionModal: "Select queried entity"
                     abs_term = LamAbs(var=selected_entity, body=reading_term)
    term_utils.py    append_lambda_term(...) persists it to the lambda DB

So a question is A READING TURNED INTO A SCHEMA by making one of its entities
a parameter. Its type follows the ordinary rule of §2:

    [(lam a) t] = [a]        a question's type is its BOUND VARIABLE's type

Hence "all questions of type A" is a concrete finite lookup over the saved
abstractions — exactly what `widgets.py:_record_contains_iri` already does:

    isinstance(term, LamAbs) and term.var.iri == iri

Substituting a question for a variable during enrichment (§7.3) therefore means
hypothesising: "this position is not a bare entity — it is an instance of that
earlier, generalised reading." R1 later firing there is that hypothesis paying
off.


7.3 Enrichment
--------------

After EVERY reading step (init, contraction or reflection) the ontology set is
enriched: for each ontology and each pointer, wherever the pointed subterm is a
VARIABLE sitting in an application, a question of matching type may be
substituted in its place, yielding a further candidate ontology.

    method enrichOnts(ontologies):
        for each ont in ontologies:
            enrichOnt(ont, ont.P)

    method enrichOnt(ont(G(t), P)):
        p = headOf(P)
        if p == null: return
        let p point to subgraph G(tp)

        enrichOnt( ont(G(t)), P - p )                    # rest of the pointers

        # (a) pointed subterm in FUNCTION position of an application
        if G(t) has a subgraph G(app(tp, t2)) and tp is a variable:
            for each question qp with [qp] == [tp]:
                let G(tnew) = G(t) with G(tp) replaced by G(qp)
                let pqp point to qp
                ontologies = ontologies union { ont(G(tnew), (P - p) union {pqp}) }
                enrichOnt( ont(G(tnew)), P - p )

        # (b) pointed subterm in ARGUMENT position of an application
        if t has a subtree app(t1, tp) and t1 is a variable:
            for each question qt1 with [qt1] == [t1]:
                let G(tnew) = G(t) with G(t1) replaced by G(qt1)
                ontologies = ontologies union { ont(G(tnew), P) }
                enrichOnt( ont(G(tnew)), P - p )

[CLARIFIED — TERMINATION] The recursion is strictly on the SHRINKING pointer
set: every recursive call passes P - p, and a newly created ontology is
enriched only for the REMAINING pointers. No question is ever substituted
inside a question introduced during the same enrichment. Depth is therefore
bounded by |P| and enrichment always terminates.

Note the asymmetry between (a) and (b), which is deliberate and follows §5's
argument/function principle: in (a) the substituted question becomes a new
POSITION (pqp joins the pointer set, replacing p), whereas in (b) the question
replaces material in function position and the pointer set is left as P.


7.4 Initialisation
------------------

  after init-1 (user selects entity A):

      ontologies = { ont( G(a), {p} ) },  p pointing to the edge G(a)

  after init-2 (user resumes a saved reading R1):

      ontologies = the ontologies of R1

  Then enrich (§7.3).


7.5 Updating on a contraction step, case 1  (A -> B at p)
----------------------------------------------------------

    newOntologies = {}
    for each ont(G(t), P) in ontologies:
        take p1 from P corresponding to p in the reading

        if t contains a subtree app(ta, tb) with p1 pointing to ta,
           [ta] == A and [tb] == B:
            if R1 is applicable to G(t) at p1:        # i.e. ta is an abstraction
                let pb point to tb
                apply R1, then perform all possible BOOKKEEPING reductions
                    (rules R2, R3, R5, R6) -> G(t1)
                newOntologies = newOntologies
                                union { ont( G(t1), (P - {p1}) union {pb} ) }

    ontologies = newOntologies
    then enrich (§7.3)

The pointer moves from the abstraction ta to tb — mirroring the reading, where
contraction case 1 moves the reader from A to B.


7.6 Updating on a reflection step  (B -> C at p, p pointing to ta, [ta] == A)
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

    newOntologies = {}
    for each ont(G(t), P) in ontologies:
        take p1 from P corresponding to p in the reading; let p1 point to toa

        if toa is an abstraction
           and R4 is applicable at p1
           and t contains a subtree app(tb, tc) with [tb] == B, [tc] == C,
               [toa] == A
           and G(toa) IS ALREADY SHARED as a subterm in both tb and tc:
            apply R4, then perform all possible BOOKKEEPING reductions
                (rules R2, R3, R5, R6) -> G(t1)
            newOntologies = newOntologies union
                { ont( G(t1),
                       (P - {p1}) union { ptr to upper-LEFT  aux port edge,
                                          ptr to upper-RIGHT aux port edge } ) }

    ontologies = newOntologies
    then enrich (§7.3)

[CLARIFIED — R4 DIRECTION] The requirement that G(toa) be ALREADY shared across
both tb and tc is a PRECONDITION, as specified: an ontology qualifies only if
the sharing pre-exists. Note for implementers that this is stricter than the
reducer's own applicability condition. In `optimal_lambda/rules.py`,
`rule4_fan_fan_diff` fires whenever two fans meet principal-to-principal on
DIFFERENT main wires, and its EFFECT is to replicate each fan onto the other's
grey and black branches — i.e. the reducer CREATES duplication rather than
requiring it. So the sharing test here is an ADDITIONAL filter layered on top
of `find_redexes`, not a restatement of it, and must be checked separately.

The two new pointers land on the sharing fan-in's auxiliary ports, which in the
implementation are `grey` (upper left) and `black` (upper right) — see §4.3.


7.7 Refutation, and the empty set
----------------------------------

[CLARIFIED] Both update rules REPLACE the ontology set with newOntologies, so
an ontology in which the required rule does not fire is DISCARDED. This is
deliberate: the user's action falsifies any model that cannot accommodate it.
The set therefore shrinks by refutation (§7.5, §7.6) and grows by enrichment
(§7.3).

AN EMPTY ONTOLOGY SET IS MEANINGFUL: it says the reading has NO VALID MODEL.
This is a distinguished state and should be surfaced to the user, not treated
as an ordinary outcome.


7.8 Ontology update for contraction cases 2 and 4 — OPEN
---------------------------------------------------------

NOT YET SPECIFIED. Cases 2 and 4 graft a saved reading R1, and R1 carries its
OWN ontology set, so the two sets must be combined somehow. The obvious
candidate is a cartesian product — pair each current ontology with each of
R1's, grafting R1's ontology-term at the position, with pointers following the
reading (case 2 adopts R1's, case 4 discards them) — but that may grow the set
quickly, and the alternative of grafting only R1's plain term discards the
models R1 had itself accumulated. Left open by decision, 2026-07-29. See O10.


8. Worked example
-----------------

Let the graph contain Protein --binds--> Ligand, Protein --hasFunction-->
Function, and Gene --encodes--> Protein.

  init-1: user selects Protein.
      t = p0                          [t] = Protein
      P = {ptr(p0)}

  contraction case 4 along Gene -> Protein, at the trivial reading R_g0:
      the reader is standing at the OBJECT of this arrow, so case 4 applies,
      instantiated at R_g0 = (G(g0), {ptr(g0)}) with [g0] = Gene.
      t = app(g0, p0)                 [t] = Protein   — UNCHANGED
      P = {ptr(app(g0,p0))}
      The reader has not moved; it has recorded that this protein is arrived
      at from a gene. R_g0's pointer is discarded, which is why a trivial
      reading behaves exactly like the omitted case 3. Case 1 was not
      available here: it would have required [t1] == Gene.

  contraction case 1 along Protein -> Ligand:
      now the reader is at the SUBJECT of the arrow, so case 1 applies.
      t = app( app(g0,p0), l0 )       [t] = Ligand
      P = {ptr(that app)}
      The reader now stands at Ligand.

  reflection contrasting Ligand -> Affinity and Ligand -> Structure:
      t = app( app(l, aff), app(l, str) )
          where l = app( app(g0,p0), l0 ), shared
      P = {pB -> grey branch, pC -> black branch}
      One position reads the ligand under Affinity, the other under Structure,
      but there is a single shared occurrence of the ligand subterm.

  contraction case 1 at pB along Affinity -> Measurement:
      the grey branch becomes app(app(l,aff), meas)
      pC is untouched — the reader has advanced one of its two positions.


