10. Expressiveness: which graphs can a Reading build?
------------------------------------------------------

Scope of this section: applicative terms over variables (t ::= x | (t t)) —
no abstractions — plus sharing. Results were checked exhaustively by
enumeration up to 6 leaves.

10.1 APPLICATION SHAPES: COMPLETE with cases 1, 2 and 4 alone.

  Every binary-tree shape is reachable (Catalan: 1, 1, 2, 5, 14, 42 — none
  missing up to 6 leaves), WITHOUT case 5.

  What does the work is that cases 2 and 4 graft a saved reading's ENTIRE TERM
  at a pointer, and a saved reading may itself be arbitrarily compound. So a
  compound can be introduced in either position:

      ((x y) (z w))     save (z w) as a reading; graft it at a pointer
                        inside (x y). No joining of two readings needed.

  Contrast: cases 1 and 4 restricted to FRESH VARIABLES only are NOT complete.
  First failure at 4 leaves, exactly the shape above; missing 1 of 5 at 4
  leaves, 6 of 14 at 5, 26 of 42 at 6. The obstruction there is POINTER
  REACHABILITY — grafting a fresh variable re-points p at the new application
  node and never ADDS a pointer, so from init-1 the reader holds one pointer
  forever and can extend only along a single spine, never growing both
  children of an application into compounds. (Confirmed: allowing a pointer
  hypothetically at EVERY node makes fresh-variable grafting alone complete at
  every size. The rules are shape-complete; what they lack is access to
  positions.)

  Saved-reading grafting supplies that access WITHOUT a navigation step: the
  reader does not reach into an unread position, it brings a finished reading
  of its own to the position it already occupies. This is why the calculus can
  be both fully local (§3b) and shape-complete.

  CONSEQUENCE: case 5 was REDUNDANT. It offered direct composition of two
  finished readings as a convenience, at the cost of being the only non-local
  step. Removing it loses no expressive power (§4.2d).

10.2 SHARING: INCOMPLETE.

  Reflection is the only operation that introduces sharing, and it emits one
  fixed pattern:

      app( app(tA, b), app(tA, c) )      with tA shared

  This fixes three things at once: a shared node has EXACTLY TWO parents, both
  parents are APP nodes, and tA occupies the FUNCTION position of both. Hence
  the following sharing graphs are NOT constructible:

    (a) fan-out > 2 — one subterm with three or more distinct uses. Nested
        reflections give nested fan-ins at distinct levels, which is not the
        same graph as one node with three parents. (Whether that distinction
        is semantically meaningful depends on the intended reading of the
        fan-in level — see O8.)

    (b) a shared subterm in ARGUMENT position — no way to build app(f,s) and
        app(g,s) sharing s as the argument of both. Reflection always places
        the shared node on the left. This is the sharpest gap, because it is
        exactly the pattern optimal reduction exists to exploit: a duplicated
        ARGUMENT that should be reduced once. The applicative analogue of
        (lam x. x x) I is not constructible.

    (c) asymmetric / late sharing — the two uses must be siblings under a
        common application created by the same step. Two positions that are
        far apart in the term, or whose sharing is recognised only later,
        cannot be joined.

    (d) cyclic sharing — every step grafts new material or joins two existing
        readings, so the result is always a DAG. No operation creates a cycle.

  CANDIDATE CLOSURE (proposed, NOT part of the specification). The dual of
  reflection — a MERGE / coreference step:

      given p1, p2 in P with [t_p1] == [t_p2]
      replace both occurrences by a single shared node (fan-in)
      P1 = P with p1 and p2 replaced by one pointer to the shared node

  Reflection consumes one pointer and yields two; merge would consume two and
  yield one. It reads naturally as "these two things I have been attending to
  separately are in fact the same thing", and it is admissible under §3b (K2)
  since both operands are positions the reader already holds. With merge
  added, cases 1/4/5 give every tree and merge gives every DAG over it.

  Whether merge is wanted is a design decision, not a defect: a reader that
  can only share what it discovers by reflection is a coherent — and more
  constrained — notion than one that can identify arbitrary co-referents.

10.3 A NOTE ON WHAT THE KG CONSTRAINS.

  Shape completeness is about the space of TREE SHAPES. Which LABELLED terms
  are reachable is separately constrained by the knowledge graph: every step
  needs an arrow that exists, and by [app(a,b)] = [b] the type at a pointer is
  its rightmost leaf. So the reachable labelled terms are exactly the
  KG-realisable ones — by design, not by omission.
